"\n\n\n\n\n## Review\nIt's such a well-known landmark in Melbourne that I made sure to visit during my trip.  \n  \nEstablished in 1854, it's the oldest public library in Australia and one of the world's first free public libraries — a place with a truly remarkable history.  \n\nThe octagonal La Trobe Reading Room, completed in 1913, was reportedly the world's largest reinforced concrete dome at the time.  \nLooking down from above, the radially arranged oak desks and green-shaded reading lamps create a breathtaking visual.  \n\nThe grand and scholarly atmosphere of this place somehow stirs a genuine  just by being here.  \n\nThe inside was so quiet and solemn that we held our breath and quietly blended in among the people deep in study.  \nSurrounded by this space buzzing with academic energy, we spent our time busily picking out a  instead of reading any books.  \n\nIf you come to Melbourne, I'd recommend not just snapping photos and leaving — take a moment to quietly absorb the atmosphere of this vast historical space.  \n## Pictures\n\n\n\n\n\n\n"